The construction of the 
2-functor ${\mathscr{E}\!nrich(\mathcal{M})}\longrightarrow{\mathscr{M}\!\mathscr{M}\!od(\mathcal{M})}$
is straightforward. 
The rest can also be proved by a standard argument using the Yoneda lemma.
We sketch the argument below.

Let us focus on the characterisation of the essential image.
Suppose that $(\mathcal{C},\Phi,\overline{\phi}_\cdot,\overline{\phi})$ is a 
metamodel of $\mathcal{M}$ such that
for each $A,B\in\mathcal{C}$, the functor $\Phi_{(-)}(A,B)$
is representable.
From such a metamodel we obtain an enrichment $({\langle -, -\rangle},j,M)$ of 
$\mathcal{C}$ over $\mathcal{M}$ as follows. 
For each $A,B\in\mathcal{C}$, choose an object ${\langle A, B\rangle}\in\mathcal{M}$ and an 
isomorphism 
$\alpha_{A,B}\colon\mathcal{M}(-,{\langle A, B\rangle})\longrightarrow\Phi_{(-)}(A,B)$.
By functoriality of $\Phi$, ${\langle -, -\rangle}$ uniquely extends to a 
functor of type 
$\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{op}}\times\mathcal{C}\longrightarrow\mathcal{M}$ while making 
$(\alpha_{A,B})_{A,B\in\mathcal{C}}$ natural.
For each $C\in\mathcal{C}$, $(\overline{\phi}_\cdot)_C\colon 1\longrightarrow
\Phi_I(C,C)\cong \mathcal{M}(I,{\langle C, C\rangle})$ gives rise to a 
morphism $j_C\colon I\longrightarrow{\langle C, C\rangle}$ in $\mathcal{M}$. 
For each $A,B,C\in\mathcal{M}$, consider the function
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=-\the\dimexpr\fontdimen22\textfont2\relax ]
