\item A morphism $F\colon V\longrightarrow\mathrm{Sem}(\mathsf{P})$ in ${\mathbf{CAT}}/\mathcal{C}$;
that is, a functor 
$F\colon\mathcal{A}\longrightarrow{\mathbf{Mod}(\mathsf{P},(\mathcal{C},\Phi))}$
such that $U\circ F=V$ 
($U\colon{\mathbf{Mod}(\mathsf{P},(\mathcal{C},\Phi))}\longrightarrow\mathcal{C}$ is the 
forgetful functor).
\end{enumerate}

The correspondence between 1 and 2 is by the universality of ends
(see (\ref{eqn:structure_of_V})). 
To give $\xi$ as in 2 \emph{without} requiring naturality
in $A\in\mathcal{A}$, is equivalent to give a 
function ${\mathrm{ob}(F)}\colon 
{\mathrm{ob}(\mathcal{A})}\longrightarrow{\mathrm{ob}({\mathbf{Mod}(\mathsf{P},(\mathcal{C},\Phi))})}$
such that ${\mathrm{ob}(U)}\circ {\mathrm{ob}(F)}={\mathrm{ob}(V)}$ (see (\ref{eqn:model_of_P})). 
