\label{def:extensive_cat}
A large category $\mathcal{V}$ is \textbf{extensive}
if and only if it admits all small coproducts and for any small set $I$ and 
$I$-indexed family $(X_i)_{i\in I}$ of objects of $\mathcal{V}$,
the functor $\coprod$ in (\ref{eqn:ext_coprod}) is an equivalence of 
categories.

\label{prop:VGph_ext}
If $\mathcal{V}$ is a large category with a strict initial object $0$, then
${{\mathcal{V}}\mhyphen\mathbf{Gph}}$ is extensive.

\label{prop:VCat_ext}
If $\mathcal{V}$ is a large category with a strict initial object and finite 
products, then ${{\mathcal{V}}\mhyphen\mathbf{Cat}}$ is extensive.

\label{prop:pb_in_V_gph_and_V_cat}
Let $\mathcal{V}$ have finite limits.
A commutative square
