\label{prop:extensive_slice}
Let $\mathcal{V}$ be an extensive category. For any object $Y\in\mathcal{V}$,
the slice category $\mathcal{V}/Y$ is again extensive.

Clearly $\mathcal{V}/Y$ has small coproducts given by  $\coprod_{i\in I}(f_i\colon 
X_i\longrightarrow Y)
= ([f_i]_{i\in I}\colon $ $\coprod_{i\in I} X_i\longrightarrow Y)$.
Also note that for any object $(f\colon X\longrightarrow Y)$ of $\mathcal{V}/Y$,
the canonical functor $(\mathcal{V}/Y)/f\longrightarrow \mathcal{V}/X$ 
which maps $(h\colon (g\colon A\longrightarrow Y)\longrightarrow f)\in 
(\mathcal{V}/Y)/f$ to
$(h\colon A\longrightarrow X)\in \mathcal{V}/X$ is an isomorphism of categories.
For any small set $I$ and $I$-indexed family $(f_i\colon X_i\longrightarrow 
Y)_{i\in 
I}$
of objects of $\mathcal{V}/Y$, the diagram 
\[
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=-\the\dimexpr\fontdimen22\textfont2\relax ]
      \node(0) at (0,1.5) {$\prod_{i\in I}((\mathcal{V}/Y)/f_i)$};
      \node(1) at (5,1.5) {$(\mathcal{V}/Y)/[f_i]_{i\in I}$};
      \node(2) at (0,0) {$\prod_{i\in I}(\mathcal{V}/X_i)$};
      \node(3) at (5,0) {$\mathcal{V}/(\coprod_{i\in I}X_i)$};

      \draw [->] 
            (0) to node (t)[auto,labelsize] {$\coprod$} 
            (1);
      \draw [->] 
            (1) to node (r)[auto,labelsize] {$\cong$} 
            (3);
      \draw [->] 
            (0) to node (l)[auto,swap,labelsize] {$\cong$} 
            (2);
      \draw [->] 
            (2) to node (b)[auto,labelsize] {$\coprod$} 
            (3);  
\end{tikzpicture}
\]
(in which the vertical arrows are the canonical isomorphisms mentioned above)
commutes. 
Since the lower $\coprod$ is an equivalence by the assumption, so is the upper 
one.

